+{"ts":1768725907309,"seq":15794,"type":"session.update","session_id":"01KF7T27BXD9SNG1ZR4PV63S57","data":{"iteration":1,"update":{"_meta":{"claudeCode":{"toolResponse":{"status":"completed","prompt":"Run /review against PR #40","agentId":"aea9861","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Perfect! Now let me compile my comprehensive review:\n\n---\n\n# PR #40 Review: Task Completion Guardrails\n\n## Summary\nThis PR adds interactive prompts when completing tasks to ensure proper documentation and spec alignment. The implementation provides guardrails that prompt users to update spec implementation status and reminds them about acceptance criteria coverage.\n\n**Branch:** `feat/task-completion-guardrails`  \n**Files Changed:** `src/cli/commands/task.ts` (+43 lines)  \n**Tests:** All passing ✅  \n**Build:** TypeScript compilation successful ✅\n\n---\n\n## Implementation Analysis\n\n### What This PR Does\n\nThe PR adds interactive prompts to the `kspec task complete` command that:\n\n1. **Prompts for spec status update** (AC-1): When completing a task with a `spec_ref`, asks the user if they want to update the linked spec's implementation status to \"implemented\"\n2. **Shows AC reminder** (AC-2): If the spec has acceptance criteria, displays a count and reminder to verify coverage\n3. **Skips for non-spec tasks** (AC-3): Only triggers for tasks with `spec_ref` set\n\n### Code Location\n\nThe new code is added in `/home/chapel/Projects/kynetic-spec/src/cli/commands/task.ts` at lines 543-584, immediately after the existing automatic sync logic (lines 525-541).\n\n---\n\n## Strengths\n\n### 1. **Proper Code Annotations**\nThe implementation includes excellent AC traceability:\n```typescript\n// AC: @task-completion-guardrails ac-1\n// Prompt to update spec implementation status to 'implemented'\n```\nThis follows the project's established pattern for linking code to acceptance criteria.\n\n### 2. **JSON Mode Handling**\nCorrectly suppresses interactive prompts in JSON mode:\n```typescript\nif (foundTask.spec_ref && !isJsonMode()) {\n```\nThis prevents breaking automated workflows or scripts that use `--json` output.\n\n### 3. **Consistent with Existing Patterns**\nThe readline usage matches other prompts in the codebase (task delete, inbox commands). The pattern is established and well-tested.\n\n### 4. **Dogfooded Implementation**\nThe PR description notes that the feature was tested on itself - completing `@task-task-completion-guardrails` triggered the prompt to update `@task-completion-guardrails`. This is excellent self-hosting validation.\n\n### 5. **User Experience**\n- Clear prompt text with default choice indicated `[y/N]`\n- Warning emoji `⚠` for the AC reminder\n- Proper pluralization (\"criterion\" vs \"criteria\")\n- Informative success message after update\n\n### 6. **Spec Alignment**\nAll three acceptance criteria are addressed:\n- ✅ AC-1: Prompt shown for spec-linked tasks\n- ✅ AC-2: AC count reminder displayed when applicable\n- ✅ AC-3: No prompt for tasks without spec_ref\n\n---\n\n## Concerns & Issues\n\n### 1. **CRITICAL: Duplicate Logic with Auto-Sync**\n\n**This is the primary issue with this PR.**\n\nLines 525-541 already implement automatic spec status syncing via `syncSpecImplementationStatus()`. This function:\n- Calculates expected status based on ALL linked tasks\n- Updates spec status if it differs from expected\n- Provides the same \"synced to implemented\" feedback\n\nThe new prompt (lines 543-584) runs **after** the auto-sync and:\n- Asks if user wants to update to \"implemented\" \n- **Always sets to \"implemented\" if user says yes**, regardless of other tasks\n- Can create **status inconsistencies**\n\n**Example Problem Scenario:**\n```\nSpec: @api-client (has 3 tasks)\n- Task 1: completed\n- Task 2: completed  \n- Task 3: in_progress\n\nComplete Task 1:\n1. Auto-sync sees \"some completed, some in_progress\" → sets spec to \"in_progress\" ✓\n2. User sees prompt: \"Update @api-client to implemented? [y/N]\"\n3. User says yes → Spec forcibly set to \"implemented\" ✗\n4. Result: Spec says \"implemented\" but Task 3 is still in_progress\n```\n\nThis **contradicts** the existing alignment system's logic. The `AlignmentIndex.calculateExpectedStatus()` method (in `/home/chapel/Projects/kynetic-spec/src/parser/alignment.ts`) already determines the correct status based on all linked tasks.\n\n### 2. **Question: What Problem Does This Solve?**\n\nThe auto-sync (lines 525-541) already:\n- Updates spec status automatically\n- Uses proper logic considering all tasks\n- Shows feedback message\n- Can be disabled with `--no-sync`\n\n**What gap does the interactive prompt fill?** Some possibilities:\n- **User confirmation**: Maybe auto-sync is too aggressive and users want control?\n- **AC reminder timing**: Perhaps the AC warning is the real value, not the status update?\n- **Override capability**: Maybe this is intentionally allowing manual override of calculated status?\n\nThe PR description doesn't explain the **why** - just the **what**.\n\n### 3. **Interaction with `--no-sync` Flag**\n\nThe auto-sync respects `--no-sync`:\n```typescript\nif (options.sync !== false && foundTask.spec_ref) {\n```\n\nBut the new prompt doesn't check `options.sync`. This means:\n```bash\nkspec task complete @task --no-sync\n# Auto-sync skipped ✓\n# Interactive prompt STILL appears ✗\n```\n\n**Is this intentional?** If a user explicitly requests `--no-sync`, should they still see the interactive prompt?\n\n### 4. **User Experience Friction**\n\nThe prompt adds an **interactive step to every spec-linked task completion**. In a workflow where you're completing multiple tasks:\n```bash\nkspec task complete @task-1 --reason \"Done\"\n# Prompt: Update @spec? [y/N] \nkspec task complete @task-2 --reason \"Done\"\n# Prompt: Update @spec? [y/N]\nkspec task complete @task-3 --reason \"Done\"\n# Prompt: Update @spec? [y/N]\n```\n\nThis could become tedious, especially since **auto-sync is already happening automatically**.\n\n### 5. **Missing Test Coverage**\n\nThe PR description notes:\n> - [x] Manual testing with task that has spec_ref and ACs\n> - [x] Manual testing with task without spec_ref (no prompt shown)\n\nBut there are **no automated tests** for this feature. Given the interactive nature, this is understandable but risky. Consider:\n- Integration tests that mock stdin/stdout\n- Unit tests for the logic flow\n- Edge case tests (multiple tasks, different statuses, etc.)\n\n### 6. **No `--force` or `--confirm` Skip Option**\n\nOther interactive prompts in the codebase support `--force` to skip confirmation (see task delete). This prompt doesn't, which could break automation or scripted workflows.\n\n---\n\n## Questions for Clarification\n\n1. **Design Intent**: What problem is this solving that auto-sync doesn't? Is this meant to:\n   - Provide user control over automatic behavior?\n   - Serve as a reminder/guardrail even when auto-sync works?\n   - Allow manual override of calculated status?\n\n2. **Relationship with Auto-Sync**: Should this:\n   - Replace auto-sync (make it opt-in via prompt)?\n   - Complement auto-sync (only prompt when sync would change status)?\n   - Override auto-sync (run after and allow manual correction)?\n\n3. **`--no-sync` Behavior**: Should the prompt respect `--no-sync` or is it intentionally separate?\n\n4. **Multi-Task Specs**: How should this behave when a spec has multiple tasks? The auto-sync logic handles this correctly by checking all tasks. Should the prompt:\n   - Only appear when completing the LAST task?\n   - Show context about other tasks?\n   - Use the same logic as auto-sync?\n\n5. **AC Reminder Value**: Is the AC count reminder the primary value here, with the status prompt being secondary?\n\n---\n\n## Recommendations\n\n### Option A: Remove Status Prompt, Keep AC Reminder\n\n**If the primary value is the AC reminder**, consider:\n\n```typescript\n// Only show AC reminder, don't prompt for status update\nif (foundTask.spec_ref && !isJsonMode()) {\n  const specResult = index.resolve(foundTask.spec_ref);\n  if (specResult.ok && specResult.item) {\n    const specItem = items.find(i => i._ulid === specResult.ulid);\n    if (specItem?.acceptance_criteria?.length > 0) {\n      const count = specItem.acceptance_criteria.length;\n      console.log(`\\n⚠ Linked spec ${foundTask.spec_ref} has ${count} acceptance criteri${count === 1 ? 'on' : 'a'} - verify they are covered\\n`);\n    }\n  }\n}\n```\n\nThis provides the reminder without introducing status conflicts. The auto-sync handles status updates correctly based on all tasks.\n\n### Option B: Make Prompt Conditional on Sync Result\n\n**If you want to keep the prompt**, make it respect the auto-sync logic:\n\n```typescript\n// Only prompt if auto-sync didn't already set to 'implemented'\nif (foundTask.spec_ref && !isJsonMode() && syncResult) {\n  if (syncResult.newStatus !== 'implemented') {\n    // Show prompt explaining why it's not 'implemented'\n    // and ask if they want to override\n  }\n}\n```\n\n### Option C: Replace Auto-Sync with Interactive Prompt\n\n**If user control is the goal**, remove or make auto-sync opt-in, and use the prompt as the primary mechanism. This would require:\n- Updating the default behavior\n- Documenting the change\n- Potentially adding a `--auto-sync` flag for the old behavior\n\n### Option D: Smart Prompting\n\n**Only show the prompt when it matters**:\n\n```typescript\n// Calculate what auto-sync would do\nconst alignmentIndex = new AlignmentIndex(updatedTasks, items);\nalignmentIndex.buildLinks(index);\nconst expectedStatus = alignmentIndex.calculateExpectedStatus(specItem._ulid);\n\n// Only prompt if this is the last task AND status would become 'implemented'\nif (expectedStatus === 'implemented' && currentStatus !== 'implemented') {\n  // Show prompt with context: \"All tasks completed. Update spec to implemented?\"\n}\n```\n\n---\n\n## Suggested Changes\n\n### 1. **Respect `--no-sync` flag**\n```typescript\nif (foundTask.spec_ref && !isJsonMode() && options.sync !== false) {\n```\n\n### 2. **Add `--force` or `--yes` flag to skip prompt**\n```typescript\n.option('--yes', 'Skip confirmation prompts')\n\n// In the code:\nif (!options.yes) {\n  // show prompt\n}\n```\n\n### 3. **Show context about other tasks**\n```typescript\nconst otherTasks = allTasks.filter(t => \n  t.spec_ref === foundTask.spec_ref && \n  t._ulid !== foundTask._ulid\n);\n\nif (otherTasks.length > 0) {\n  const inProgress = otherTasks.filter(t => t.status === 'in_progress').length;\n  console.log(`Note: ${otherTasks.length} other task(s) linked to this spec (${inProgress} in progress)`);\n}\n```\n\n### 4. **Use the calculated expected status, don't hardcode \"implemented\"**\n```typescript\n// Instead of always setting 'implemented', use alignment logic\nconst expectedStatus = alignmentIndex.calculateExpectedStatus(specItem._ulid);\n\nconst answer = await new Promise<string>((resolve) => {\n  rl.question(`\\nUpdate ${foundTask.spec_ref} status to ${expectedStatus}? [y/N] `, resolve);\n});\n\nif (answer.toLowerCase() === 'y') {\n  await updateSpecItem(ctx, specItem, {\n    status: {\n      maturity: currentStatus.maturity,\n      implementation: expectedStatus, // Use calculated, not hardcoded\n    },\n  });\n}\n```\n\n---\n\n## Minor Issues\n\n### 1. **Import Style Inconsistency**\nThe code uses dynamic import for readline:\n```typescript\nconst readline = await import('readline');\n```\n\nWhile the file already has top-level imports. This is fine but worth noting - likely done to avoid importing readline when not needed.\n\n### 2. **No Error Handling for stdin**\nIf stdin is closed or unavailable (e.g., running in a non-interactive environment despite JSON mode being off), the readline could fail. Consider wrapping in try-catch.\n\n### 3. **Grammar Edge Case**\nThe pluralization is correct, but the article could be \"a\" or \"an\" depending on pronunciation preference:\n```typescript\nconsole.log(`\\n⚠ Linked spec has ${count} acceptance criteri${count === 1 ? 'on' : 'a'} - verify they are covered\\n`);\n```\n\n\"...has 1 acceptance criterion\" sounds slightly awkward. Consider \"...has acceptance criteria defined (1 total)\" or similar.\n\n---\n\n## Conclusion\n\n**Code Quality:** ✅ High - well-structured, follows patterns, good annotations  \n**Feature Implementation:** ⚠️ Partial - implements AC but conflicts with existing auto-sync  \n**Test Coverage:** ❌ None - only manual testing  \n**Breaking Changes:** ⚠️ Potential - adds interactive step to existing command\n\n### Recommendation: **Request Changes**\n\nThe PR implements the stated acceptance criteria but introduces a **fundamental design conflict** with the existing auto-sync system. The interactive prompt can override the calculated status from auto-sync, creating alignment inconsistencies.\n\n**Before merging**, clarify:\n1. The intended relationship between auto-sync and the interactive prompt\n2. How this should behave with multi-task specs\n3. Whether the AC reminder alone provides sufficient value without the status prompt\n\n**Suggested Path Forward:**\n1. Discuss design intent with the author\n2. Either:\n   - Remove status prompt, keep AC reminder (simplest, safest)\n   - Make prompt respect auto-sync logic and only show when meaningful\n   - Refactor to replace auto-sync entirely (breaking change, needs discussion)\n3. Add automated tests\n4. Add `--yes` flag for automation\n5. Respect `--no-sync` flag\n\nThe PR is close to being excellent - it just needs alignment (pun intended) with the existing status tracking system.\n\n---\n\n## Files Referenced\n\n- `/home/chapel/Projects/kynetic-spec/src/cli/commands/task.ts` (modified)\n- `/home/chapel/Projects/kynetic-spec/src/parser/alignment.ts` (context)\n- `/home/chapel/Projects/kynetic-spec/.kspec/` (spec definitions)"}],"totalDurationMs":268086,"totalTokens":38769,"totalToolUseCount":17,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":141,"cache_read_input_tokens":35062,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":141,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":3564,"service_tier":"standard"}},"toolName":"Task"}},"toolCallId":"toolu_0153wY7CTLDv6it6necfUtqN","sessionUpdate":"tool_call_update"}}}
