rectly suppresses interactive prompts in JSON mode:\n```typescript\nif (foundTask.spec_ref && !isJsonMode()) {\n```\nThis prevents breaking automated workflows or scripts that use `--json` output.\n\n### 3. **Consistent with Existing Patterns**\nThe readline usage matches other prompts in the codebase (task delete, inbox commands). The pattern is established and well-tested.\n\n### 4. **Dogfooded Implementation**\nThe PR description notes that the feature was tested on itself - completing `@task-task-completion-guardrails` triggered the prompt to update `@task-completion-guardrails`. This is excellent self-hosting validation.\n\n### 5. **User Experience**\n- Clear prompt text with default choice indicated `[y/N]`\n- Warning emoji `⚠` for the AC reminder\n- Proper pluralization (\"criterion\" vs \"criteria\")\n- Informative success message after update\n\n### 6. **Spec Alignment**\nAll three acceptance criteria are addressed:\n- ✅ AC-1: Prompt shown for spec-linked tasks\n- ✅ AC-2: AC count reminder displayed when applicable\n- ✅ AC-3: No prompt for tasks without spec_ref\n\n---\n\n## Concerns & Issues\n\n### 1. **CRITICAL: Duplicate Logic with Auto-Sync**\n\n**This is the primary issue with this PR.**\n\nLines 525-541 already implement automatic spec status syncing via `syncSpecImplementationStatus()`. This function:\n- Calculates expected status based on ALL linked tasks\n- Updates spec status if it differs from expected\n- Provides the same \"synced to implemented\" feedback\n\nThe new prompt (lines 543-584) runs **after** the auto-sync and:\n- Asks if user wants to update to \"implemented\" \n- **Always sets to \"implemented\" if user says yes**, regardless of other tasks\n- Can create **status inconsistencies**\n\n**Example Problem Scenario:**\n```\nSpec: @api-client (has 3 tasks)\n- Task 1: completed\n- Task 2: completed  \n- Task 3: in_progress\n\nComplete Task 1:\n1. Auto-sync sees \"some completed, some in_progress\" → sets spec to \"in_progress\" ✓\n2. User